WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.428
In lesson one you saw the login. Now the pact between the app and Keycloak: where each of the two writes its addresses, what happens when they disagree, and what is really inside a token. The lab is already installed.

00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:33.225
The app is one Python file. On its side it knows three things: who the issuer is, Keycloak; who it is, lab-web; and the addresses it wants to come back to, after login and after logout.

00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:49.994
On Keycloak's side, the card of the client lab-web says the same things: the return addresses allowed after login, and after logout. They must match letter for letter.

00:00:50.094 --> 00:00:59.366
And under Credentials, the secret the app uses to introduce itself to Keycloak: the same one written in the Python file.

00:00:59.466 --> 00:01:13.971
From the command line the same card is read with kcadm: first the client's id, then its two addresses.

00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:24.630
Let us break the pact. I change the allowed return address: no longer callback, but altrove. The app, on its side, knows nothing about it.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:38.573
Login from the app: Keycloak stops before even asking for a password. Invalid redirect address. The access code never goes to an address that was not registered.

00:01:38.673 --> 00:01:44.183
I put callback back, and the pact holds again.

00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:50.822
Login as Alice: in again.

00:01:50.922 --> 00:02:02.888
From inside, the app shows the claims of Alice's token, already verified: who she is, who issued it, for whom, until when, and her roles.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:18.260
Logout goes through Keycloak too: the app sends the browser there with the token in hand, Keycloak closes the session and sends it back to the registered exit address. Without that address, it would stop.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:33.209
Now let us open a token. The service token in the lab lives five seconds, too few to look at it: with kcadm I give it five minutes. This too is one line in the client's card.

00:02:33.309 --> 00:02:50.033
From the client I ask for a token and look at it raw: a long string, three pieces separated by a dot. Header, payload, signature. Here are the first sixty characters of each.

00:02:50.133 --> 00:03:04.361
The first piece is just base64, not encrypted: it says which algorithm signed it and with which key, kid. The key the app fetches from the discovery.

00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:20.086
The second piece is the content, readable by anyone too. Issued by, for whom, by which app, when, until when, and the roles. A token hides nothing: it is no place for secrets.

00:03:20.186 --> 00:03:31.620
So I forge one: I rewrite the content with one more hour of life, and put it back between the same header and the same signature.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:49.033
I ask Keycloak about both tokens: the real one is active, the forged one is not. The signature covers the content: change one character and it no longer matches. That is why the app can trust what it reads.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:04:00.481 line:6%
In the guide: exercises 3, 4 and 7, discovery and claims, the login and logout round trip, the errors.
